  "releaseNotes": "Patch release for Entity Framework Core 10 on .NET 10.\n\nKey values\n- Doris has no auto-increment, but entities no longer have to say so. EF Core's inferred\n  ValueGenerated.OnAdd is turned off while the model is finalized, so an integer key needs no\n  ValueGeneratedNever(). Guid keys keep the client-side generator EF Core gives them and now work.\n- Asking for a generated value explicitly is still refused, but later and more precisely:\n  HasDefaultValueSql, HasDefaultValue and HasComputedColumnSql fail model validation, while\n  ValueGeneratedOnAdd() and [DatabaseGenerated(Identity)] fail at SaveChanges, naming the column.\n\nVectors\n- float[] maps to an ARRAY<FLOAT> column instead of falling back to a STRING column holding JSON\n  text, which is the only shape that can carry an ANN index. There is deliberately no LINQ surface for\n  the distance functions: Doris uses an ANN index only for ORDER BY l2_distance_approximate(...)\n  LIMIT k, so nearest-neighbour search is written as raw SQL.\n\nQuery translation\n- Fixed DateTime literals. They were rendered as DATETIME '...', which the Doris parser rejects, so\n  any query that inlined a DateTime constant failed at the server. They are now TIMESTAMP '...', the\n  typed-literal spelling Doris accepts, and the value is still a DATETIME.\n\nJSON documents\n- Every scalar member type is covered by the same two shapes: json_extract_string for text, and\n  CAST(json_extract_string(...) AS store type) for int, long, double, decimal, bool, DateTime and\n  DateOnly. Nested documents come back through plain json_extract.\n\nDocumentation\n- docs/scripts/ carries runnable probes for the server behaviour the provider relies on, including\n  the DATETIME literal rejection and the ANN index contract.",
